sweep_summaries.summary:
ANGLE: standards, regulation and industry practice. I compared what the rules REQUIRE about assumed kinematic limits with what published methods PROVIDE.

QUERIES (WebSearch, standard and extended): IEEE 2846-2022 / 2846a-2025 / 3321-2024 scope; R157 minimum following distance, Annex 3 and the careful-and-competent driver; R152 moving target and false reaction; ISO 34502 reasonably-foreseeable ranges; ISO 15622 time-gap limits; R171 DCAS following distance and string instability; UN ADS regulation in-service monitoring (ISMR); UL 4600 SPIs and statistical monitoring; Mobileye RSS parameter setting; Bosch/Continental/ZF statements; Baidu Apollo RSS; adaptive or situation-aware RSS; RSS calibration from naturalistic data; mu-ODD switching; e-process / conformal test martingale with a switch to a conservative controller; CUSUM car-following; FDA PCCP versus automotive; queue warning versus safety margin; WaveX, I-24 MOTION and OpenACC metadata.

PRIMARY TEXTS I READ MYSELF (EUR-Lex HTML, grepped locally; scratch /tmp/claude-1000/-srv-shared-Workspace-phd-phd-speed-harmo-v5b1/59738c75-cfe8-434e-99ee-b78b7087de37/scratchpad/wf8):
- R157 original text (https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=CELEX:42021X0389):
  - 5.1.1: no collisions 'reasonably foreseeable and preventable';
  - 5.1.9: a 'process to manage the safety and continued compliance ... over lifetime';
  - 5.2.3.3: d_min = v x t_front, with t_front from 1.0 s at 7.2 km/h to 1.6 s at 60 km/h. The 01-series extension to 130 km/h was not read [VERIFY];
  - 5.2.5.1: avoid a collision with a leader braking 'up to its full braking performance' if d_min was kept;
  - 5.2.5.2: cut-in visible at least 0.72 s;
  - Annex 4 App. 3 driver model: 0.4 s risk perception ('driving simulator data in Japan'), 0.75 s, 0.6 s jerk to 0.774 G ('NHTSA and Japan'), 0.85 G from R152, 0.375 m lateral wandering, max lateral speed 'real world data in Japan'.
- R152 02 series (OJ L 2024/2497, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=OJ:L_202402497): the moving target travels at a constant 20 km/h. I found no braking-leader test. False-reaction avoidance needs manufacturer evidence.
- R171 DCAS (OJ L 2025/1899, https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/HTML/?uri=OJ:L_202501899):
  - 2.31 defines 'String Instability';
  - 5.3.7.2.5.1: 'anticipatory behaviour ... stable, low-amplitude dynamics';
  - 5.3.7.2.5.2: 'strategies aiming to address significant string instability'. No metric or test: the word 'string' appears only twice;
  - base test: decelerating target up to 4 m/s2 at 50 km/h;
  - 7.1: an in-service monitoring programme;
  - the regulation is 'subject to continuous review' from monitoring data.
- Mobileye, 'Implementing the RSS Model on NHTSA Pre-Crash Scenarios' (https://static.mobileye.com/website/corporate/rss/rss_on_nhtsa.pdf): the parameter values 'should be determined through collaborative discussions amongst the industry and automotive standards-bodies, likely facilitated by regulators.'

IEEE PAGES (read):
- 2846-2022 sets a 'minimum set of reasonable assumptions'. The WG page says sources of uncertainty such as prediction or perception errors are out of scope (https://sagroups.ieee.org/2846).
- 2846a-2025 adds scenarios and road users (lane changes, merging). Status: approved, pending publication.
- 3321-2024 (published 2025-04-28, https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/3321/11080/) gives methods to identify reasonably foreseeable values, update them as a scenario evolves, and validate the selected assumptions. Full text not read [VERIFY].
- Certad et al. (https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.16530) imply that 2846 gives a method, not numbers.

FIELD PICTURE:
- Assumed limits are set by consensus or regulation, as fixed worst-case values (R157 'full braking'; RSS by agreement).
- Data-driven work is offline range setting:
  - Nakamura et al. 2022, SAKURA data, DOI 10.1109/ACCESS.2022.3162601 [abstract via search, VERIFY];
  - de Gelder & Op den Camp, TNO (https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02177, https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01117);
  - Xu et al. 2021 RSS calibration (TR-C 123:102988) [VERIFY].
- Validation of R157's benchmark driver (Olleja, Markkula, Bärgman 2025, AAP 213:107922, https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/543008) shows the Annex 3 models differ from humans in 38 SHRP2 cut-in near-crashes.
- Runtime adaptation of RSS exists but has no statistical trigger or violation-rate guarantee:
  - Salvi et al. ITSC 2022, DOI 10.1109/ITSC55140.2022.9922192 (Fraunhofer with Intel authors): hard switches between parameter sets can create critical situations;
  - Li et al. CAiSE 2023: risk-aware choice of a parameter set;
  - VLM-CASE 2026 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05180).
- In-service monitoring is now required:
  - R171 7.1;
  - UN ADS regulation ISMR, adopted by WP.29 in June 2026 per UNECE press snippets (fetch blocked) [VERIFY];
  - UL 4600 SPIs. The SPI literature (Ratiu et al., https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.00578) gives argument patterns but no statistical test.

BIG PLAYERS:
- Mobileye/Intel (RSS; Jack Weast chaired P2846). The 2020 kickoff minutes list NVIDIA, ARM, Infineon, Baidu, NXP, Exponent, Intel and Qualcomm; Bosch intended to join; Waymo was undecided [search snippet, VERIFY].
- TNO (de Gelder).
- JAMA / SAKURA (ISO 34502).
- Chalmers (Bärgman).
- JRC (Ciuffo, Makridis: OpenACC, DCAS string stability).
- Waymo (NIEON, Collision Avoidance Testing; blog snippets only [VERIFY]).
- Koopman (UL 4600, mu-ODD).

COMPANIES:
- Baidu said it would integrate RSS into Apollo (Intel press release, https://www.intc.com/news-events/press-releases/detail/145/baidu-to-integrate-mobileyes-responsibility-sensitive).
- I found no public statement from Bosch, Continental, ZF, CARIAD or BYD on how assumed kinematic limits are set or validated (search: 'Bosch OR Continental OR ZF ... reasonably foreseeable ... RSS adoption').

CERTIFICATION:
- R157 fixes both the floor (the time-gap table) and the leader assumption (full braking).
- So a learned b_hat below full braking cannot shorten an ALKS gap below d_min. Certifiable adaptation must be one-sided above the floor.
- Reasoning: a certifiable bounded adaptation needs:
  1. a finite set of pre-verified parameter values;
  2. one-sided movement relative to the regulatory or certified floor;
  3. a trigger with a stated false-alarm rate that stays valid under continuous monitoring;
  4. rate-limited transitions (the Salvi finding);
  5. an audit trail usable for ISMR / R171 7.1;
  6. a fail-safe default when inputs are missing.
- This mirrors the FDA PCCP for AI devices (final guidance Dec 2024: planned modifications, a methodology and an impact assessment; https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-12-04/html/2024-28361.htm, not read in full [VERIFY]). I found no automotive PCCP analogue (search 'predetermined change control plan automotive OR automated driving').

NEURAL NETWORKS (on ChatGPT's claim): in every crack below, the auditable decision logic should stay simple: a ladder plus a statistical test. A NN can at most supply a risk covariate that the test guards. A NN is not where the gap lies.

